{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1402 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "2ee0a657505b7531fae4039e9126be9cfdc44838fb0abb35a73171480da994b1" :edb/db-before-t 1401 :edb/db-after-t 1402 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534714 50 #inst "2026-10-08T04:52:15.667Z" 13194139534714 true] [17592186105367 1100 "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\n- The number of tickets sold for a randomly selected baseball game in a certain stadium\n\n- The length of a randomly selected baseball field.\n\n- The weight, in grams, of a slice cut from a cake weighing $1\\,\\text{kg}$" 13194139534714 true] [17592186105367 1100 "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\n1. The number of tickets sold for a randomly selected baseball game in a certain stadium\n2. The length of a randomly selected baseball field.\n3. The weight, in grams, of a slice cut from a cake weighing $1\\,\\text{kg}$" 13194139534714 false] [17592186105367 1145 "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/assets/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are the integers $0,1,2,3,\\ldots,$ which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\n- Statement II describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real numbers in the interval $(0, \\infty).$\n\n- Statement III describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real numbers in the interval $(0, 1\\,000].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534714 true] [17592186105367 1145 "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1347/Source/Images/0018f659e35a580657f383181bfabb0d.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are the integers $0,1,2,3,\\ldots$, which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n- Statement II describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real numbers in the interval $(0, \\infty)$.\n- Statement III describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real numbers in the interval $[MATH: (0, 1\\,000].]$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534714 false] [17592186105368 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/assets/26d09b87ebbabe4c1e1dcd8d5442b1d3efce216d7f404808a3777b8ffa3304ff.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8}({x}^{2}+1),\\, & -1<x\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^1 f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x = 1$\n\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" 13194139534714 true] [17592186105368 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n${\\frac{3}{8}(x^{2} + 1),\\begin{vmatrix}-1 < x \\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1347/Source/Images/3c1b36806fc8009a30d99e37f36e5336.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x$\n2. $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^1 f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x = 1$\n3. $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" 13194139534714 false] [17592186105368 1145 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S$, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x$\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{S} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x$.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n$∫_{-1}^{1}f(x)dx|= ∫_{-1}^{1}\\frac{3}{8}(x^{2} + 1)dx; = \\frac{3}{8}∫_{-1}^{1}x^{2} + 1dx; = \\frac{3}{8}[\\frac{x^{3}}{3} + x]_{-1}^{1}; = \\frac{3}{8}([\\frac{1^{3}}{3} + 1] - [((-1)^{3})/(3) + (-1)]); = \\frac{3}{8}([\\frac{1}{3} + 1] - [- \\frac{1}{3} - 1]); = \\frac{3}{8}(\\frac{4}{3} - (-\\frac{4}{3})); = \\frac{3}{8} \\cdot \\frac{8}{3}; = 1$.\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139534714 false] [17592186105368 1145 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{S} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{3}{8}({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+x]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}([\\frac{{1}^{3}}{3}+1]-[\\frac{(-1)^{3}}{3}+(-1)]) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}([\\frac{1}{3}+1]-[-\\frac{1}{3}-1]) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}(\\frac{4}{3}-(-\\frac{4}{3})) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}\\cdot \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139534714 true] [17592186105377 1100 "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{3}+k{x}^{2},\\, & 0<x<1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534714 true] [17592186105377 1100 "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n${2x^{3} + kx^{2},\\begin{vmatrix}0 < x < 1, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$." 13194139534714 false] [17592186105377 1145 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S$, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{S} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{1} (2x^3+kx^2) \\, \\textrm dx = 1\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{1}(2x^{3} + kx^{2})dx &= [\\frac{x^{4}}{2} + \\frac{kx^{3}}{3}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= [\\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{k}{3}] - 0 \\\\\n&= \\frac{k}{3} + \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac k3+\\dfrac{1}{2} = 1 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad k=\\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$" 13194139534714 false] [17592186105377 1145 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{S} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{1} (2x^3+kx^2) \\, \\textrm dx = 1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{x}^{3}+k{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}+\\frac{k{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{k}{3}]-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{3}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac k3+\\dfrac{1}{2} = 1 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad k=\\dfrac{3}{2}.\n$$" 13194139534714 true] [17592186105781 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305032 13194139534714 true] [17592186105781 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305095 13194139534714 true] [17592186105781 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305102 13194139534714 true] [17592186105781 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305109 13194139534714 true] [17592186105782 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305039 13194139534714 true] [17592186105782 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305046 13194139534714 true] [17592186105782 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305053 13194139534714 true] [17592186105782 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305060 13194139534714 true] [17592186105782 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305067 13194139534714 true] [17592186105807 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305074 13194139534714 true] [17592186105807 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305081 13194139534714 true] [17592186105807 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305088 13194139534714 true] [17592186105807 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305116 13194139534714 true] [17592186106456 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534714 true] [17592186106456 1100 "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\n- The percent concentration of fruit concentrate in a randomly selected fruit drink\n- The number of times a coin is flipped until it lands on tails\n- The number of days in a particular week" 13194139534714 true] [17592186106456 1100 "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\n1. The percent concentration of fruit concentrate in a randomly selected fruit drink\n2. The number of times a coin is flipped until it lands on tails\n3. The number of days in a particular week" 13194139534714 false] [17592186106456 1145 "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/assets/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $[0,100]$ (since the concentration can be anywhere between $0%$ and $100%$).\n\n- Statement II does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are the integers $1,2,3,…,$ which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\n- Statement III does *not* describe a continuous random variable. The number of days in a particular week is not the outcome of a trial or experiment. (By definition, there are $7$ days in a week.)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534714 true] [17592186106473 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534714 true] [17592186106473 1100 "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k}{{x}^{2}},\\, & 3\\le x\\le 9, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534714 true] [17592186106473 1100 "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$f(x) = {\\frac{k}{x^{2}},\\begin{vmatrix}3 \\le x \\le 9, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$." 13194139534714 false] [17592186106473 1145 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{9}\\frac{k}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{9}\\frac{k}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3}^{9}k{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{k}{x}]}_{3}^{9} \\\\  & =-\\frac{k}{9}+\\frac{k}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2k}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2k}{9}=1\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{9}{2}.\n$$" 13194139534714 true] [17592186305032 1017 #edb/ref 17592186305033 13194139534714 true] [17592186305032 1089 "q-126053" 13194139534714 true] [17592186305032 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534714 true] [17592186305032 1100 "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\n- The volume, measured in cubic centimeters, of water in a cup of coffee\n- The average length, measured in millimeters, of hairs on a horse\n- The number of hairs on a cat" 13194139534714 true] [17592186305032 1113 #uuid "78edb6bd-ed48-54d3-90d2-5d11cd8b4ff4" 13194139534714 true] [17592186305032 1145 "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/assets/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are the integers $0,1,2,3,…,$ which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534714 true] [17592186305033 1000 #edb/ref 17592186305034 13194139534714 true] [17592186305033 1000 #edb/ref 17592186305035 13194139534714 true] [17592186305033 1000 #edb/ref 17592186305036 13194139534714 true] [17592186305033 1000 #edb/ref 17592186305037 13194139534714 true] [17592186305033 1000 #edb/ref 17592186305038 13194139534714 true] [17592186305033 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534714 true] [17592186305033 1041 "selection" 13194139534714 true] [17592186305033 1104 #edb/ref 17592186305037 13194139534714 true] [17592186305033 1110 #uuid "cc281f46-91de-5d1b-9764-36cdc503c98f" 13194139534714 true] [17592186305034 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534714 true] [17592186305034 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534714 true] [17592186305034 1111 #uuid "172da751-b7b1-5993-92ce-0b3a89fc22b6" 13194139534714 true] [17592186305035 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534714 true] [17592186305035 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534714 true] [17592186305035 1111 #uuid "cba6ca18-28a9-5486-94cd-df83dc6c91fa" 13194139534714 true] [17592186305036 1034 "I only" 13194139534714 true] [17592186305036 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534714 true] [17592186305036 1111 #uuid "c5612fe7-40d4-514a-8685-6d64f1a2fd2f" 13194139534714 true] [17592186305037 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534714 true] [17592186305037 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534714 true] [17592186305037 1111 #uuid "790cf3d3-633d-5625-b997-6c1db9d21bc8" 13194139534714 true] [17592186305038 1034 "II only" 13194139534714 true] [17592186305038 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534714 true] [17592186305038 1111 #uuid "fe2bbd98-032e-589e-acc1-f778f65eceac" 13194139534714 true] [17592186305039 1017 #edb/ref 17592186305040 13194139534714 true] [17592186305039 1089 "q-158419" 13194139534714 true] [17592186305039 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534714 true] [17592186305039 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/assets/4acac98ef7276cb1e90557ca161666a33ccf0a7627b9ebfcbbe44508b9e5f5bb.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2},\\, & 0<x\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" 13194139534714 true] [17592186305039 1113 #uuid "5c694066-1106-59d2-ad9b-3f01367d7aea" 13194139534714 true] [17592186305039 1145 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{1}^{3}}{2}]-[\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{0}^{3}}{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139534714 true] [17592186305040 1000 #edb/ref 17592186305041 13194139534714 true] [17592186305040 1000 #edb/ref 17592186305042 13194139534714 true] [17592186305040 1000 #edb/ref 17592186305043 13194139534714 true] [17592186305040 1000 #e